   The room was going under.
   I could feel it leave people. Nothing dramatic. A shoulder dropping.
A man who’d been laughing at the champagne going quiet in the
middle of the laugh. On the Garden every color was draining toward
the platform where the forty sat, and through them, down, toward an
empty pedestal the size of a cake stand.
   I leaned my shoulder against the steel column. It was cold. I was
holding on to it as though it were a railing on a ship.
   The drill hall at three in the morning, 1952. The great room dark, the
roof lost in dark. No white carpet. Bare planks, scuffed, with a long bar
of moonlight lying across them from a high window. A woman in a
gray work dress pushed a wide mop down the length of the hall in the
quiet.
   She was singing.
   I couldn’t make out the words. Something in Spanish, maybe, or
Kreyòl, or both, something her mother had sung, with no tune you
could write down. She wasn’t on any beat. She went slower, then faster,
then stopped to push a bucket, then took the song up again from some‐
where in the middle. She sang to the planks and the steel and the cold.
She had no audience. Nobody in the world was counting what she did.
   The floor remembered her.
   I don’t know how else to say it. In the hundred years that room had
held drill and count and ceremony, and every pair of boots hitting the
planks together, it had held one woman at three in the morning,
singing off the beat for nobody. And that was in the wood too. It was in
there with all the rest.
   Nobody had ever put it in a ledger.
   The moonlight faded. The count came back. Seven. Eight. I was
standing at a steel column holding my own breath at the pace of a man
leading me.
   I let it out wrong.



